UPSTREAM WORK THIS PROJECT DEPENDS ON — DOC-06
==============================================

Splitstick did not invent a protocol. It adopts formats and ideas that other
people designed, tested in public and documented honestly, and it is a plugin
rather than a fork so that stays true. Credit is a licence condition for some
of these and a matter of accuracy for all of them.

open-multi-agent / @open-multi-agent/core (MIT)
    The run-journal primitive Splitstick collects through, and the project
    whose README says plainly where its own guarantee stops: "It proves
    lineage and content, not that the file was never edited." Projects that
    oversell do not write that section. Splitstick is the layer that closes
    that specific gap, and OMA's constraint is principled rather than an
    oversight.
    https://github.com/open-multi-agent/open-multi-agent

C2SP — Community Cryptography Specification Project
    The signed-note and tlog-checkpoint formats, and the tlog-witness
    protocol. Checkpoints, cosignatures and the witness interaction are C2SP's
    design, not ours; adopting them is what lets a third party implement a
    witness without asking us anything.
    https://c2sp.org

Sigstore, and Rekor
    The transparency-log model this project anchors into, and the working
    demonstration that public, append-only, independently-witnessed logs are
    operable at scale rather than only publishable. The Rekor adapter here
    submits to infrastructure the Sigstore community runs and pays for; it is
    opt-in precisely because a ten-second cadence against a public good is not
    acceptable use.
    https://www.sigstore.dev  ·  https://github.com/sigstore/rekor

Trillian, and transparency-dev / witness
    Trillian is the Merkle-tree log implementation the transparency-log
    ecosystem was built on, and transparency-dev/witness is the stateful
    witness whose refusal to cosign a shortened history is the only mechanism
    that closes the attack this project exists for. The adapter in
    packages/witness-tlog speaks its protocol.
    https://github.com/google/trillian  ·  https://github.com/transparency-dev/witness

Bruce Schneier and John Kelsey
    "Secure Audit Logs to Support Computer Forensics" (1999) — the forward-
    secure hash-chained audit log, and the argument that a log's integrity must
    survive the compromise of the machine that wrote it. The epoch ratchet in
    packages/core/src/ratchet.mjs is their construction; the honest statement
    of its precondition, which this project is careful about, is also theirs:
    they named the custody assumption rather than hiding it.
    https://www.schneier.com/academic/paperfiles/paper-auditlogs.pdf

RFC 8785 (JSON Canonicalization Scheme), RFC 6962 (Certificate Transparency),
RFC 3161 (Time-Stamp Protocol), and the OpenTimestamps project
    The canonical encoding, the Merkle tree and its inclusion and consistency
    proofs, the timestamp-token format, and the free public calendar that
    serves as the roll-up anchor no company can withdraw.
    https://opentimestamps.org
